[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-01", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 01 — round trips, and a hypothesis the measurement refuted", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-01.edn", :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 01 — round trips, and a hypothesis the measurement refuted\n\n> Seed: \"advance ayatori's maturity, efficiency and performance with the\n> co-scientist approach\". The loop is Generate -> Reflect -> Rank -> Evolve ->\n> Meta (ADR-2606141500, ported in `90-docs/design-quality/coscientist.cljc`).\n> What makes it not theatre is that the judge is a measurement: ADR-2607132300\n> records an LLM panel scoring four libraries 4.0-5.0/5 while missing four\n> concrete gaps none of them named.\n\n## The judge\n\n`bench/fitness.cljk`. Counts, never wall clock -- ADR-2608160100 sets the\nmetric as round trips, and this workstation runs many agents at once, so a\nmillisecond figure would describe the machine. Two arms answer the SAME query\nover the SAME snapshot, and the harness exits 2 if they ever disagree on the\nanswer, so a faster arm cannot win by returning less.\n\nThe corpus holds the answer size fixed at 3 rows while the database grows, so\nthe sweep is a statement about the database and not about the result.\n\n## Baseline (2026-09-04)\n\n    subj  quads  blocks  rows | per-object  disc  read     bytes\n      50    100       4     3 |          6     3     3     13868\n     800   1600      23     3 |         12     6     6    109764\n\nDatabase grew 16x; round trips grew 2.00x. So the persistent path is NOT\nO(database) -- it follows the index ranges the query names, as claimed. That\nis the good news, and it is now measured rather than asserted.\n\nTwo things stood out instead:\n\n  - `discoveries` equalled `block-reads` at every size. One IPNI lookup per\n    block, on top of the fetch. Confirmed separately: a fetch-fn that ignores\n    the provider entirely -- which is exactly what `pack-fetcher` does -- still\n    paid N discoveries whose results were discarded.\n  - bytes grew 7.9x for a 16x database while reads grew 2x. Transfer, not\n    round trips, is what scales here at this size.\n\n## Generate\n\n  H1  Discovery is unconditional. A block already in hand still costs an IPNI\n      lookup. Predicted: -N round trips for packed reads.\n  H2  Bytes scale faster than reads, so transfer is the cost to watch.\n  H3  memo-hits is 0 within a single query; the memo only pays across queries.\n  H4  `range-open` asks for a fixed read-ahead window per block because the\n      CARv2 index stores offsets and not lengths.\n\n## Reflect\n\nH1 is additive and backward compatible (a new optional `:block-source`, absent\n= today's behaviour) -- low risk. H4 needs no new data: the index records are\nsorted, so each frame ends where the next begins, and the header already gives\nthe payload end. H3 is a property of the workload, not a defect. H2 is not a\nhypothesis on its own; it is the measurement H4 explains.\n\n## Rank / Evolve\n\nH1 first, because it is measurable in isolation:\n\n    without block-source   discoveries 6   fetch-attempts 6   verified 6\n    with    block-source   discoveries 0   fetch-attempts 0   verified 6\n\nThen the end-to-end arm, which is where the loop earned its keep -- H1 shipped\nand the measurement PARTLY REFUTED it:\n\n    at 800 subjects   per-object   12 round trips   109,764 bytes\n                      packed        8 round trips   394,217 bytes\n\nRound trips fell 1.5x and transfer rose 3.6x. Packing was a win on the metric\nthe ADR names and a loss on the one that actually scales. A loop whose judge\nwas an opinion would have shipped that.\n\nH4 was the evolved hypothesis, and it is what fixed it. `bounded-index` derives\neach frame length from the next record's offset -- no extra fetch, and the\nderived lengths are asserted equal to the ones `pack` itself computed:\n\n    at 800 subjects   per-object       12 round trips   109,764 bytes\n                      packed (window)   8               394,217\n                      packed (exact)    8               110,996\n\n## Meta\n\nResult: 1.50x fewer round trips at parity transfer (+1.1%, which is the header\nand index reads and is bounded, not proportional).\n\nTwo findings the loop produced that were not hypotheses:\n\n  - The synchronous `open-snapshot` returns `#{}` under nbb when handed a\n    Promise-returning `blind-fn` -- and `arrangement.core/commit!` REQUIRES\n    one on cljs, so following its contract and then opening synchronously is\n    the natural way to arrive there. A query that COULD NOT RUN returned what\n    a query that ran and matched nothing returns. It now refuses, naming\n    `open-snapshot-async`.\n  - `read-block` destructured the wrong key and returned nil for every block\n    while the round-trip test stayed green, because `b/equal?` answers true\n    for two nils. Every byte comparison now passes a floor first.\n\nSeed for iteration 02:\n\n  - `:block-source` is threaded through the SYNCHRONOUS getter only. A Worker\n    still pays discovery per block behind a pack; the async path needs the\n    source awaited and coalesced with `:in-flight`.\n  - The arms are measured against an in-memory range server. Round trips are\n    transport-independent, but the byte figures are not a network measurement.\n  - Packing policy is untouched: this measures reading a pack, and nothing in\n    production writes one.  [WITHDRAWN 2026-09-09, later the same day] The correction that stood here was itself wrong, and the original sentence above is RIGHT. It said `net-kotobase/engine` carries the pack namespaces and sets KOTOBASE_PACK_WRITES=1 in its testnet env, and concluded pack writes were LIVE there. The first half is true and the conclusion does not follow: a source tree is not a deployment. Measured from outside with no credential -- backend.kotobase.net/_health answers 503 for three missing secrets (KOTOBASE_B2_KEY_ID, KOTOBASE_B2_APP_KEY, KOTOBA_SEED), and kotobase-cf-wasm-testnet.aozora.app/_health answers 200 with fields (canonical_cloudflare_independent, object_store) that exist only in net-kotobase/control-plane/kotobase-graph-database's worker, a tree with NO pack namespaces at all. Both trees declare an env.testnet with the same worker name, so a deploy from either replaces the other, and control-plane deployed last. So nothing in production or testnet writes or reads a pack, and these iterations are evidence for a plane that is still unwired. Root ADR-2608170300 carries the measurement."}]
